NAME
       copy, paste - a clipboard shared by every terminal

SYNOPSIS
       copy
       copy file ...
       copy -l
       copy -c

       paste [n]
       paste -l
       paste -i

DESCRIPTION
       Text copied in one terminal can be pasted in any other. Without
       this there is no way to move text between the twelve consoles,
       since there is no pointer to select with.

       copy takes what is typed until Ctrl-D, or the contents of the
       files named, or whatever is piped into it. paste writes it to
       standard output, so it goes wherever it is sent: to the screen,
       into a file, or through another command.

       The last ten things copied are kept. paste 1 is the one before
       last, paste -l lists them with a preview of each, and paste -i
       shows the same list to choose from.

       The file manager copies the path of the highlighted file with y,
       into this same clipboard.

EXAMPLES
       ifconfig | copy              take the output
       copy /etc/passwd             take a file
       paste                        look at it
       paste > saved.txt            into a file
       paste 2 | grep error         an older one, through something
       paste -i                     pick from the list

NOTES
       The clipboard lives in /tmp, so it is shared by everything on
       the machine, including a session arriving over ssh, and is empty
       again after a reboot.

SEE ALSO
       cfm(1), neo(1), csh(1)
